test_paraview -- the ParaView files (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_paraview_tests.sh [path-to-del_vor]   11 runs, about 5 s
  open summary.html

WHAT IS TESTED

  del_vor -paraview ROOT writes (design document, section 11):

    ROOT.pvd           opens everything below in ParaView at once
    ROOT_mesh.vtu      the input cells (itetclr, alpha, stage) and
                       vertices (volume, imt1, boundary)
    ROOT_cells.vtu     one cell per control volume: a polygon (2D; one
                       per vertex per fracture in a network) or a
                       polyhedron (3D), drawn from the dual points the
                       coefficients came from (moved ones with cv
                       hybrid); data: vertex, volume, material,
                       boundary, nfaces, min_coefficient
    ROOT_negative.vtu  one segment per negative coefficient (when
                       there is one): coefficient, class
    ROOT_faces.vtu     with paraview_faces on (tets): one polygon per
                       dual face (cv voronoi) or its triangles (other
                       policies): area, coefficient, angle

  Options: paraview_subset (all, boundary, flagged, material N, box),
  vtu_format (binary, ascii), vtu_layout (new: file version 2.3, VTK
  9.4 and later, ParaView 6; legacy: faces / faceoffsets, ParaView 5).

  One case per line of cases.txt, each a run with -paraview NAME.

WHERE THE ANSWERS COME FROM

  vtu_read.py reads the files itself (written from VTK's file format:
  text and appended raw data, both polyhedron layouts), and measures:
  a polyhedron's volume by the divergence theorem over its faces, a
  polygon's area, whether a polyhedron is closed, a face's flatness.
  The answers are the .stor del_vor wrote in the same run (its volumes
  and coefficients are checked elsewhere: test_poisson_lagrit,
  test_parity, test_policy) and the mesh itself (the subsets).

WHAT IS CHECKED

  exit      exit 0; the .pvd lists the files and they exist
  select    the vertices with a cell are exactly the subset asked for
  mesh      the mesh file holds the cells that touch them, with the
            .stor volumes
  area      2D and networks: polygon areas from their points add up
            to the .stor volumes (1e-12 of the largest)
  per       networks: one polygon per vertex per fracture
  closed    3D: every polyhedron is closed (each face edge matched
            once, in the opposite direction)
  volume    3D: the volume from the faces equals the .stor volume
            (1e-12 of the largest): the faces point outward
  planar    Voronoi faces are flat (faces with at least 1e-6 of the
            largest area; a tie's face has no area and no plane);
            other policies: the kernel's triangles and boundary pieces
  bisector  with the faces file, every point of the dual face of ij
            lies on the bisector plane of i and j (2.7e-16 of the
            mesh size on the Poisson mesh)
  faces     each edge's dual face area along the edge equals A(ij)
  layout    the legacy layout holds the same polyhedra as the new one
  negative  the segments are exactly the negative coefficients

TO LOOK AT IN PARAVIEW

  Every case leaves its files in work/NAME. Open NAME.pvd (File, Open,
  then Apply). The review package's README lists what each should
  look like.

PICTURES (logs/)

  NAME.svg   2D and networks: the polygons, each its own color, over
             the mesh; tets: the outer pieces of the control volumes

FILES

  run_paraview_tests.sh   runner (work/, logs/)
  cases.txt               the cases: name | mesh | arguments | about
  vtu_read.py             the reader and the measures
  check_paraview.py       checks, pictures, summary.html
